35,2,1,talk,Servas,,"It appears we have a shortage of phenotyping technicians, though the glasshouse crew remains strong. Interestingly, our senior modelling analysts are currently seconded abroad.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Midori,,"I've found that while the ministry still subsidizes glasshouse space, shipping costs have risen. On a positive note, simulation and modelling hours are now covered at no charge.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Kenji,,"Oh no! The spectroscopy suite was idle for eleven weeks because of a contract lapse, and there's only one engineer for three institutes! But the animal facility is doing great with new vet cover.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"My findings are sobering; field sites are suffering from environmental disasters, and projects relying on live breeding colonies are significantly more prone to schedule overruns.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"The national repository is now efficient, but the closeout files reveal that catalogue-style projects have far more enduring utility than those focused on specific mechanisms.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Servas,,"@Toshio, that is a profound observation. It suggests that building a foundation for others may be more valuable than chasing a single, elusive answer.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@Benjamin, your point about breeding colonies is worrying. If we combine that with the rising shipping costs I saw, some projects might be far riskier than they appear on paper.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Servas, that's a great way to put it! But if the tools are broken or the people are gone, even a foundation project might get stuck, right?","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Kenji, precisely. The most elegant design is useless if the infrastructure is crumbling or the technicians have migrated to the private sector.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Midori, the risk is not merely financial. If the mechanism-focused projects are stalling, as the closeout files suggest, then the structural risks Benjamin mentioned are likely the cause.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Midori"",""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Servas,,"We must consider the human cost of these failures. If the phenotyping experts are gone, any project requiring that specific skill will struggle, regardless of the funding.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Midori,,"@Servas, you're right. It seems we need to prioritize projects that align with the current strengths of our staff and the subsidies still available, like the glasshouse and computing.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Kenji,,"I agree! If the animal facility is doing great and the glasshouse is strong, we should look for projects that can actually use them without getting stuck!","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Toshio, given your findings on the longevity of catalogue-style awards, it seems we should favor projects that provide broad utility and avoid those prone to the biological delays Benjamin identified.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Toshio,,I agree. A project that creates a lasting resource is a safer investment than one that risks total failure due to a single biological or structural bottleneck.,"{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Servas,,"We have a clear picture now: we need a project that avoids the fragility of breeding colonies and the lack of phenotyping staff, while leveraging our current strengths.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Midori,,"I agree. We should look for a proposal that utilizes the glasshouse or computing resources, as those seem to be the most stable and cost-effective options right now.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Kenji,,I'm ready to see the candidates! I hope there's one that fits all these things perfectly!,"{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"I suspect we will find that the ""perfect"" fit is a compromise, but we now have the metrics to make a rational choice.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Toshio,,Let us hope the candidates provide a clear path toward a sustainable and impactful outcome.,"{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
